3.2 Manifold Structure of the State Spaces

The human and AI state spaces are not merely Cartesian products of vector spaces; several components carry nontrivial differential-geometric structure that constrains the dynamics.

Definition 3.1 (Memory Manifold \mathcal{M}). The episodic memory state is modeled in the finite-dimensional Hilbert space \mathcal{M} = \mathbb{R}^{n_M} equipped with the standard Euclidean metric g_M(u,v) = u^\top v. The tangent space at any point is canonically isomorphic to \mathbb{R}^{n_M}.

Definition 3.2 (Knowledge Graph Stratified Space \mathcal{K}). The personal knowledge graph K(t) = (V_K, E_K, \varphi, \psi) with temporal edge weights w: E_K \to [0,1] \times \mathbb{R}_+ is embedded into a fixed-dimensional vector space via a graph neural network encoder \Psi: \mathcal{K} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_K} with the following properties: \Psi is injective on isomorphism classes of graphs up to a maximum node count N_{\max}, and \Psi is Lipschitz with respect to the graph edit distance d_{\text{edit}}. We define the metric on \mathcal{K} as d_K(K_1, K_2) = \|\Psi(K_1) - \Psi(K_2)\|_{\mathbb{R}^{n_K}}. This renders \mathcal{K} a metric space isometrically embeddable in \mathbb{R}^{n_K}, and we identify T_K\mathcal{K} with the tangent space of the embedding.

Definition 3.3 (Values Simplex \mathcal{V}). Let N_v be the cardinality of the value vocabulary \Omega_{\text{values}}. The values state space is the open probability simplex:

\mathcal{V} = \operatorname{int}(\Delta^{N_v-1}) = \left\{ V \in \mathbb{R}^{N_v} : V_i > 0, \sum_{i=1}^{N_v} V_i = 1 \right\}

equipped with the Fisher-Rao Riemannian metric. For tangent vectors u, v \in T_V\mathcal{V} = \{u \in \mathbb{R}^{N_v} : \sum_i u_i = 0\}, the metric is:

g_V(u, v) = \sum_{i=1}^{N_v} \frac{u_i v_i}{V_i}

Lemma 3.1 (Geometry of the Values Simplex). (\mathcal{V}, g) is a Riemannian manifold of constant negative sectional curvature -1/4, isometric to the positive orthant of the sphere S^{N_v-1} of radius 2 via the diffeomorphism \phi: \mathcal{V} \to S_+^{N_v-1} given by \phi(V)_i = 2\sqrt{V_i}. Under this metric:

1. The geodesic distance between V, W \in \mathcal{V} is d_{FR}(V, W) = 2 \arccos\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N_v} \sqrt{V_i W_i}\right).
2. For any fixed p \in \mathcal{V}, the function f(V) = \frac{1}{2}d_{FR}^2(V, p) is geodesically 2-convex: along any unit-speed geodesic \gamma(s), we have \frac{d^2}{ds^2}f(\gamma(s)) \geq 2.
3. The Riemannian gradient and Hessian are given by:

\operatorname{grad}_V f = \Pi_V(\nabla f), \quad \operatorname{Hess}_V f(u, v) = u^\top (\nabla^2 f) v - \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i} \frac{u_i v_i}{V_i^2} \frac{\partial f}{\partial V_i}

where \Pi_V: \mathbb{R}^{N_v} \to T_V\mathcal{V} is the orthogonal projection (\Pi_V u)_i = u_i - V_i \sum_j u_j with respect to the Euclidean metric.

Proof. The isometry to the sphere is standard in information geometry; see Amari and Nagaoka (2000, Section 2.5). The sphere of radius 2 has sectional curvature 1/4. The geodesic distance formula follows from the spherical distance under the isometry. The 2-convexity property follows because, on a manifold of constant curvature -1/4, the Hessian of squared distance satisfies \operatorname{Hess}_V(\frac{1}{2}d_{FR}^2) \succeq g_V by comparison geometry (Petersen, 2006, Corollary 6.5.3). \square

Definition 3.4 (Goal Manifold \mathcal{G}). The goal state is a hierarchical poset with four temporal layers. We formalize \mathcal{G} as a product of weighted sets:

\mathcal{G} = \{(G_s, G_m, G_l, G_t) \in 2^{\Omega_s} \times 2^{\Omega_m} \times 2^{\Omega_l} \times 2^{\Omega_t}\}

subject to the coherence constraint G_s \preceq G_m \preceq G_l \preceq G_t where \preceq denotes instrumental consistency. We embed \mathcal{G} into \mathbb{R}^{n_G} via an order-preserving embedding that assigns to each goal set a vector of softmax-normalized weights. The metric is the induced Euclidean metric on the embedding.

Definition 3.5 (Skills Vector Space \mathcal{S}). \mathcal{S} = [0,1]^{n_S} with the standard Euclidean metric. For dynamics, we extend to \mathbb{R}^{n_S} and project onto [0,1]^{n_S} as needed.

Definition 3.6 (Total Human State Manifold). \mathcal{H} = \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{K} \times \mathcal{V} \times \mathcal{G} \times \mathcal{S} equipped with the product Riemannian metric. The tangent bundle is T\mathcal{H} = T\mathcal{M} \times T\mathcal{K} \times T\mathcal{V} \times T\mathcal{G} \times T\mathcal{S}, and a vector field on \mathcal{H} is a section of this bundle.

Definition 3.7 (AI State Manifold). The AI state space \mathcal{A} = \hat{\mathcal{A}} \times \hat{\mathcal{C}} \times \mathcal{E} \times \Lambda where \hat{\mathcal{A}} \cong \mathcal{H} (the AI's model space), \hat{\mathcal{C}} is a discrete log space with metric induced by edit distance on sequences, \mathcal{E} \cong S^+(n_H) (positive semidefinite cone), and \Lambda \cong \mathbb{R}^{n_\Lambda}.

3.3 The Coupled Dynamical System

Definition 3.8 (HALSC Coupled System). The human-AI lifelong symbiotic companion system is defined by the coupled Itô stochastic differential equations on \mathcal{H} \times \mathcal{A}:

dH(t) = f(H(t), A(t), E(t))\,dt + \sigma_H(H(t), A(t))\,dW_H(t) \tag{3.1}

dA(t) = g(H(t), A(t), E(t))\,dt + \sigma_A(H(t), A(t))\,dW_A(t) \tag{3.2}

where:

· H(t) = (M(t), K(t), V(t), G(t), S(t)) \in \mathcal{H} is the human state.
