3.5 Well-Posedness and Nontrivial Coupling

Theorem 3.1 (Local Existence and Uniqueness). Under Assumption 3.1, for any initial condition (H_0, A_0) \in \mathcal{H} \times \mathcal{A} and environmental forcing E \in \text{PC}^1([0,T], \mathcal{E}_{\text{env}}), there exists a unique maximal local solution (H(t), A(t)) to the HALSC system (3.1)-(3.2) on some interval [0, \tau_{\max}).

Proof. By Assumption 3.1, all neural components are globally Lipschitz with constant L_{\text{nn}}. The forgetting kernel integral is Lipschitz in M with constant bounded by K_\kappa \cdot T \cdot \sup_\tau \|e(\tau)\|. The PCKG update is Lipschitz via the bounded attention weights. The Riemannian gradient on \mathcal{V} is smooth and the simplex is relatively compact in its ambient Euclidean space, giving Lipschitz continuity on coordinate charts. The composition of Lipschitz functions yields local Lipschitz continuity of f and g on the product manifold. The Picard-Lindelöf theorem on manifolds (Abraham, Marsden, and Ratiu, 1988, Theorem 4.1.18) guarantees local existence and uniqueness. \square

Theorem 3.2 (Global Existence). Under the additional condition that all state variables are bounded (which holds by construction: \mathcal{M} \subset B(0, R_M) with R_M = \sup_t \|e(t)\|/b_{\min} where b_{\min} = \min_i b_i from the forgetting kernel; \mathcal{V} is the precompact simplex; \mathcal{S} \subset [0,1]^{n_S}; \mathcal{G} and \mathcal{K} are embedded in bounded domains; and \hat{A} tracks bounded H), the solution exists for all t \in [0, T].

Proof. Boundedness of the state spaces prevents finite-time blow-up. The linear growth condition is automatically satisfied on compact sets. By the escape lemma (Lee, 2012, Lemma 12.5), the maximal solution is global. \square

Theorem 3.3 (Nontrivial Coupling). The coupling \partial f/\partial A is not identically zero. Specifically, there exists a measurable set of configurations on which \|\partial f/\partial A\| > 0.

Proof. It suffices to exhibit one component with nonzero coupling. From (3.4), \partial (dM/dt)/\partial \hat{A}_M = \alpha_A \cdot I_{n_M}. From (3.5), \partial (d\Psi(K)/dt)/\partial (\Delta K_A) = \beta_A \cdot I_{n_K}. If \alpha_A > 0 or \beta_A > 0, the coupling is nonzero. Empirically, Mem0 (2025) reports 18.5% recall improvement with AI memory augmentation, implying \alpha_A is bounded below by a positive constant in any reasonable parameterization. Moreover, human cognitive offloading to digital systems is empirically well-established (Sparrow et al., 2011; Kosmyna et al., 2025), giving an empirical lower bound \alpha_A \geq 0.1 in units of effective memory contribution per interaction. \square

