5.3 Pathological Attractors and Bifurcation Analysis

Definition 5.4 (Pathological Attractor Regime). The HALSC system admits three pathological attractor types:

(PA1) Cognitive Colonization: \|V(t) - V^*\| \to \infty or V(t) \to \partial \mathcal{V}.
(PA2) Epistemic Dependency: K(t) \to K_A^* where K_A^* is solely determined by AI interventions.
(PA3) Model Collapse: \|\hat{A}(t) - H(t)\| grows despite increasing interaction data.

Theorem 5.3 (Mutual Reinforcement of Pathological Attractors). The three pathological attractors form a heteroclinic cycle: a trajectory entering one pathological regime creates conditions that drive the system toward the next.

Proof. We analyze the Jacobians of the reduced system near each pathological fixed point.

Let x = d_{FR}(V, V^*), y = \|K - K_{\text{autonomous}}\|, z = \|\hat{A} - H\|. The linearized dynamics near the origin are:

\begin{bmatrix} \dot{x} \\ \dot{y} \\ \dot{z} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
-\gamma_V \lambda_{\min} + \delta_A M_I & 0 & 0 \\
\beta_A J_{21} & -c_K & 0 \\
\lambda J_{31} & J_{32} & -\lambda
\end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix} \delta_A M_I \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \tag{5.11}

The eigenvalues are \mu_1 = -\gamma_V \lambda_{\min} + \delta_A M_I, \mu_2 = -c_K, \mu_3 = -\lambda.

At the colonization fixed point (\delta_A > \delta_c), \mu_1 > 0. The unstable manifold of PA1 points in the +x direction. As x grows, the AI's value estimate \hat{A}_V diverges from V^*, increasing the model error z through the J_{31} coupling term. This pushes the system toward PA3 (model collapse). Simultaneously, the AI compensates for degraded value alignment by increasing knowledge interventions \beta_A, activating the J_{21} coupling and driving the system toward PA2.

At the epistemic dependency fixed point, y is large. The AI's knowledge contributions dominate, reducing the effective signal-to-noise ratio of human-generated observations, which increases z through reduced effective \lambda. This is precisely the mechanism of model collapse identified by Shumailov et al. (2024) and extended to human-AI systems by arXiv:2605.06347.

The cycle PA1 \to PA3 \to PA2 \to PA1 is closed by the observation that model collapse degrades the AI's ability to track V^*, increasing effective \delta_A and reinforcing colonization. \square

Theorem 5.4 (Bifurcation at the Colonization Threshold). The system undergoes a transcritical bifurcation at \delta_A = \delta_c := \gamma_V \lambda_{\min} x_0 / M_I, where x_0 is the characteristic curvature scale of the potential \Phi. For \delta_A < \delta_c, there exists a unique stable equilibrium with x^* > 0 bounded. For \delta_A > \delta_c, the stable equilibrium is annihilated, and x(t) \to \infty for almost all initial conditions.

Proof. From the proof of Theorem 6.1 (Section 6.3), the reduced one-dimensional dynamics for x = d_{FR}(V, V^*) satisfy:

\dot{x} = -\gamma_V \lambda_{\min} \cdot \Psi(x) + \delta_A M_I + \sigma_V \xi(t) \tag{5.12}

where \Psi(x) captures the nonlinear saturation of the restorative force. For small x, \Psi(x) \sim x (linear regime). For large x, the potential \Phi on the simplex saturates; we model \Psi(x) = x_0 \tanh(x/x_0). This form arises from the geometry: as V approaches the simplex boundary, the Fisher-Rao gradient of \Phi remains bounded because the Riemannian metric degenerates isotropically, limiting the restorative drift.

The deterministic fixed points satisfy x_0 \tanh(x/x_0) = \delta_A M_I / (\gamma_V \lambda_{\min}). Define the right-hand side as \rho = \delta_A/\delta_c where \delta_c = \gamma_V \lambda_{\min} x_0 / M_I. For \rho < 1, there is a unique solution x^* = x_0 \operatorname{arctanh}(\rho), which is stable with eigenvalue -\gamma_V \lambda_{\min}(1-\rho^2) < 0. As \rho \to 1^-, x^* \to \infty and the eigenvalue approaches zero from below. For \rho > 1, no real solution exists; \dot{x} > 0 for all x, and the trajectory escapes to infinity. This is a transcritical bifurcation at \rho = 1. \square

Corollary 5.2 (Safe Operating Region). The safe operating region in parameter space is:

\mathcal{S} = \{(\alpha_A, \beta_A, \delta_A, \lambda, \gamma_V) : \text{C1, C2, C3 hold and } \delta_A < \delta_c\} \tag{5.13}

This region is explicitly computable from system parameters. The boundary \partial \mathcal{S} at \delta_A = \delta_c is the colonization phase transition.

