APPENDIX C: NUMERICAL METHODS FOR HALSC SIMULATION

C.1 Discretization Schemes

The HALSC dynamics contain both ordinary and stochastic differential equations on Riemannian manifolds. We present discretization methods that preserve the geometric constraints.

C.1.1 Euler–Maruyama for Euclidean Components. For components living in Euclidean spaces (\mathcal{M}, \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{G}, \Lambda, and the embeddings of \mathcal{K} and \mathcal{A}), the standard Euler–Maruyama scheme is used:

X_{n+1} = X_n + f(X_n) \Delta t + \sigma(X_n) \sqrt{\Delta t} \,\xi_n,

with \xi_n \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I). Strong order of convergence is 1/2.

C.1.2 Geodesic Euler–Maruyama for the Values Simplex. To simulate V(t) on \mathcal{V} with the Fisher–Rao metric, we use a geometric Euler scheme that respects the manifold structure. In the Stratonovich formulation (6.1), the update is:

V_{n+1} = \operatorname{Exp}_{V_n}\!\big( -\gamma_V \operatorname{grad}_V \Phi(V_n, E_n) \Delta t + \delta_A \Pi_{V_n} I_A(V_n, \hat{A}_{V,n}) \Delta t + \sigma_V \Pi_{V_n} \sqrt{\Delta t} \,\xi_n \big),

where \operatorname{Exp}_p: T_p\mathcal{V} \to \mathcal{V} is the Riemannian exponential map. On the simplex, this can be computed efficiently via the sphere isometry: map to the sphere, move along a great circle for distance equal to the norm of the tangent vector, then map back. This scheme is manifestly constraint-preserving and has strong order 1/2.

C.1.3 PCKG Update. The knowledge graph is updated via the GNN embedding \Psi. After each time step, the embedding is advanced by the Euler step; then graph reconstruction from the embedding is performed by a learned decoder (not required for prediction purposes, as we work directly in embedding space).

C.1.4 TPE Prediction. The trajectory prediction engine uses the Neural SDEs; during simulation, we sample multiple trajectories with the same Euler–Maruyama steps to estimate the predictive distribution.

C.2 Error Bounds and Convergence

Theorem C.1 (Discretization Error). For a globally Lipschitz SDE on a compact Riemannian manifold, the mean-square error of the geodesic Euler–Maruyama scheme satisfies

\mathbb{E}\left[ \sup_{0 \leq n \leq N} d^2(X_{n\Delta t}, X(t_n)) \right]^{1/2} \leq C \Delta t^{1/2},

where the constant C depends on the Lipschitz constants of the drift and diffusion and the curvature bounds of the manifold.

Proof. This is a standard result for geometric numerical integration on manifolds; see Higham (2001) and the extension to Riemannian manifolds by Malham and Wiese (2008). The key is that the exponential map is a second-order approximation to the flow, and the stochastic Taylor expansion gives the half-order strong convergence. ∎

Corollary C.1 (Simulation Horizon). To simulate the HALSC system over a time horizon T with mean-square error tolerance \varepsilon^2, one requires \Delta t = O(\varepsilon^2 / (T C^2)). For example, with T = 30 years, \varepsilon = 0.01 (in normalized state units), and typical Lipschitz constants, \Delta t \approx 1 day is sufficient for stable trajectories.

C.3 Implementation of V* Estimation

The empirical Fréchet mean \hat{V}_n^* is computed by iterative geodesic averaging (Robbins–Monro):

V^*_{k+1} = \operatorname{Exp}_{V^*_k}\!\left( \frac{\alpha_0}{1 + \beta k} \operatorname{Log}_{V^*_k}(V(t_k)) \right).

The logarithmic map on the simplex is computed via the spherical isometry:

\operatorname{Log}_p(q) = \frac{d_{FR}(p,q)}{\sin(d_{FR}(p,q))} \left( \frac{\sqrt{q/p}}{\sum \sqrt{pq}} - p \right),

where the division is elementwise and the formula holds for p not on the boundary. Convergence to V^* is assured by the strict convexity of the Fréchet function.

